<title>ITALY: Chirac attacks U.S. over trade sanctions.</title>
<headline>Chirac attacks U.S. over trade sanctions.</headline>
<dateline>NAPLES, Italy 1996-10-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>French President Jacques Chirac attacked recent U.S. trade actions aimed at isolating Cuba, Iraq and Libya and called for Europe to stand against the controversial measures.</p>
<p>&quot;The unilateral U.S. initiatives are unacceptable,&quot; Chirac told a news conference following a meeting with Italian Prime Minister Romano Prodi.  </p>
<p>Chirac attacks U.S. over trade sanctions =2</p>
<p>The United States stepped up its 35-year-old embargo of Cuba in March with the Helms-Burton act, which seeks to isolate the Communist-ruled island by curbing foreign investment there.</p>
<p>Since then, President Bill Clinton has also introduced trade curbs against Libya and Iran for alleged involvement in terrorism, pledging to penalise non-U.S. companies which have large trading links with either country.</p>
<p>-- Rome newsroom +396 6782501</p>
